body:
I am experiencing this issue, when trying to use nix-ld with flakes. Is there a solution to this?

I am using a devShell:

```nix
{
  inputs = {
    nixpkgs.url = "github:NixOS/nixpkgs/nixpkgs-unstable";
    flake-utils.url = "github:numtide/flake-utils";
  };
  outputs = {
    nixpkgs,
    flake-utils,
    ...
  }:
    flake-utils.lib.eachDefaultSystem (system:
    let
      pkgs = nixpkgs.legacyPackages.${system};
    in {
        devShell = pkgs.mkShell {
            NIX_LD = builtins.readFile "${pkgs.stdenv.cc}/nix-support/dynamic-linker";

            buildInputs = with pkgs; [
                nodePackages.pnpm
            ];
            shellHook = ''
                alias npm="pnpm"
            '';
        };
    });
}
```

@Mic92 whoa, that was quick! 

I came up with the following today:

```
# nix-ld.nix
{config, lib, pkgs, ...}:{
  programs.nix-ld.enable = true;
  environment.variables = {
      NIX_LD_LIBRARY_PATH = lib.makeLibraryPath (config.systemd.packages ++ config.environment.systemPackages);
      NIX_LD = "${pkgs.glibc}/lib/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2";
  };
}
```
I don't know excatly how the module system works, but my assumption was that for systemd.packages and environment.systemPackages to contain all configured packages, it would have to run at the very last, even after the main configuration.nix file. So in the system configuration flake, I'd import the above module after configuration.nix file like so:

```
{
  description = "My NixOS configuration";
  inputs = {
    nixpkgs.url = "github:nixos/nixpkgs/nixos-unstable";
  };
  outputs = {self,nixpkgs, }:{
    nixosConfigurations.my-machine = nixpkgs.lib.nixosSystem {
      system = "x86_64-linux";
      modules = [
         ./configuration.nix
         ./nix-ld.nix
      ];
      specialArgs = { inherit nixpkgs; };
    };
  };
}
```

But I couldn't login to gnome after this :sweat: and I discarded it. But it'd still be cool, if nix-ld would somehow detect all the libraries that are installed on the system anyway and make them available. Having a list of "common" libs is probably the better idea, but it also means that there will be stuff installed that's probably not needed.

I also use `nix-ld` with bazel, which clears env variables. In my case, an acceptable solution is to patch it on my machine:
```patch
   if (!ctx.nix_ld) {
-    log_error(&ctx, "You are trying to run an unpatched binary on nixos, but you have not configured NIX_LD or NIX_LD_" NIX_SYSTEM ". See https://github.com/Mic92/nix-ld for more details");
-    return 1;
+    ctx.nix_ld = "@dynamicLinkerPath@";
   }
```
in the NixOS configuration overlay:
```nix
final: prev: {
  nix-ld = prev.nix-ld.overrideAttrs (f: p: {
    patches = [
      (pkgs.runCommand "nix-ld.patch" {} ''
        export nixldso=$(cat '${pkgs.stdenv.cc}/nix-support/dynamic-linker')
        substituteAll ${./nix-ld.patch} $out
      '')
    ];
  });
}
```
Build times are negligible (compared to, say, nvidia drivers). If it would be packaged the same way in `nixpkgs`, it would be built by hydra with the correct dynamic linker. The challenge then is to keep the patch stable: don't change the source file, targeted by the patch, too much.

> I also use `nix-ld` with bazel, which clears env variables. In my case, an acceptable solution is to patch it on my machine:
> 
> ```diff
>    if (!ctx.nix_ld) {
> -    log_error(&ctx, "You are trying to run an unpatched binary on nixos, but you have not configured NIX_LD or NIX_LD_" NIX_SYSTEM ". See https://github.com/Mic92/nix-ld for more details");
> -    return 1;
> +    ctx.nix_ld = "@dynamicLinkerPath@";

You will run into issues with this approach if your NIX_LD_LIBRARY_PATH uses libraries linked against a different version of glibc compared to the dynamic loader (which will load it's own libc). That's why it is usually recommend to set both at the same time. I'd also recommend to test if nix-ld-rs fixes your issue as this is the solution I want to make the default for nix-ld after some testing.

